From: <your name> <your@email>
Subject: [PATCH] Pin the RSA transform to PKCS#1 v1.5

Cipher.getInstance("RSA") is provider-dependent. The JCE resolves the bare
alias against whichever provider is first, and BouncyCastle's default for it
is NoPadding.

JNKPU only uses BouncyCastle's lightweight API and never registers the
provider, so today SunJCE wins and the default happens to be PKCS1Padding.
The bug is latent, not active. But any application that embeds JNKPU and
registers BC as a provider - a one-line change elsewhere - silently turns
every unlock into garbage. No exception, no error: NoPadding is
deterministic and returns 256 bytes of noise, which then fails as a bad
client key rather than as a padding problem.

MS-NKPU is PKCS#1 v1.5. Say so.

No provider is pinned deliberately: the JCE resolves the provider at init()
from the key, so this one string works for both a file-backed key (SunJCE)
and a PKCS#11 token handle (SunPKCS11).

--- a/src/net/coagulate/JNKPU/Cryptography.java
+++ b/src/net/coagulate/JNKPU/Cryptography.java
@@
+    /** MS-NKPU uses PKCS#1 v1.5. Never use the bare "RSA" alias: its meaning
+     *  depends on which provider wins. */
+    private static final String TRANSFORM="RSA/ECB/PKCS1Padding";
+
     private static PrivateKey key=null;
@@
-            Cipher rsa=Cipher.getInstance("RSA");
+            Cipher rsa=Cipher.getInstance(TRANSFORM);
             rsa.init(Cipher.DECRYPT_MODE,key);
